## Contracts Meet Option

A stack has a critical precondition for pop: it must not be empty. Without contracts, you'd check manually every time. With contracts, the precondition is explicit and enforced:

```xiom
type Stack = {
  items: Vec[Int];
}

fn Stack.pop(self: &mut Self) -> Option[Int]
  requires: self.items.len() > 0;
  ensures: self.items.len() == self@pre.items.len() - 1;
{
  let item = self.items[self.items.len() - 1];
  self.items.remove(self.items.len() - 1);
  return Some(item);
}

fn Stack.peek(self: &Self) -> Option[Int]
  requires: self.items.len() > 0;
{
  return Some(self.items[self.items.len() - 1]);
}
```

If the caller violates `requires`, execution traps with a crystal-clear message: "Contract violation: self.items.len() > 0 failed at L4-33:pop". No segfault, no corrupted state, no mystery.

### The Synergy with Option

Why return `Option[Int]` when `requires` already prevents empty-stack calls? Because `Option` communicates to the type system that pop *might* fail — even though at this call site, the contract guarantees it won't. The `ensures` clause proves that after a successful pop, the stack shrinks by exactly one element.

